 a  no-brainer no no chance is it the blue  marsh award in 2022 when marsh died in  1936 the toronto star dedicated four  pages to its former sports editor a good  indicator as to his influence at the  time the story spoke of him as fearless  ever fair respected while ignoring the  racist comments and epithets which laced  his columns what he routinely wrote  about the great indigenous runner tom  longboat among others is unprintable  today he opposed calls to boycott the  1936 berlin games and while the star's  news pages were filled with stories  about the terrible treatment of jews in  nazi germany marsh called it an internal  german matter so you could say oh sure  that's the way they spoke in those days  no  they didn't speak that way unless  influential people like lou marsh told  them that it was okay to speak that way  forsythe isn't looking to erase marsh's  past she sees him as a historical figure  we can learn from but not necessarily  honor i hope we're at a moment in time  where we can reconsider  the name of this award  for someone who  doesn't have this kind of legacy i think  we're at a moment in time where people  are ready for this kind of change and  you know now's the time to do it perhaps  by honoring someone whose contributions  to canada go beyond sport mike relay  global news toronto  cracking the hot housing market ahead a  new way to help ease the burden of home  buying  the bank of canada is keeping its key  interest rate at a record low the bank  slashed its rate to a quarter of one  percent at the start of the pandemic  it's warning a hike could come sometime  between april and september next year  high inflation rates aren't expected to  fall until the second half of 2022  and with the rising cost of living in  this country budgets are tightening a  toronto company is offering a new way to  break into one of canada's hottest  housing markets it's called co-ownership  and gaviola explains how it works and  why some industry analysts have  reservations  with the average home price in toronto  now nearing 1.2 million dollars and the  median annual salary for people in the  gta only around 60 000 saving enough for  a down payment has never felt more out  of reach there's a large cohort of  canadians that are squeezed out of the  market and they want most of the  benefits of owning with the freedoms and  flexibilities of renting key is a  startup offering co-ownership targeting  young workers who feel priced out  one of the suites on offer is a one  bedroom plus den 600 square foot condo  in a trendy toronto neighborhood valued  at 535 000  for an initial investment of 13 500  buyers get a contract locking in a two  and a half percent equity stake from  there buyers must pay nearly two  thousand dollars monthly but can get on  the path to home ownership gradually  building equity with the option to buy  the property and take on a mortgage  after three years  industry analysts say that's significant  if your name isn't on title it shouldn't  be billed as co-ownership so it is more  like a rents to own scheme  rather than  the way they're marketing it i feel like  what they're presenting is is very very  misleading key says this is a new model  that's different from rent to own and  ownership is spelled out in a legally  binding contract and certain rights over  the property when you have an equity  stake that increases in value with the  underlying asset two you have a say in  what goes on renovations and  customization and three you share in  repairs and maintenance on a pro rata  basis he passes all those ownership  tests former bank of canada governor  stephen poloz is among keys advisors the  startup has 60 units including some in  the building behind me with more than  100 people joining the wait list every  single day it has plans to expand beyond  toronto i think this will appeal to  people that are having difficulty  learning or knowing how to save and  knowing what to do with that money but  then i would take a step back and say if  you're having that difficulty don't  become a homeowner one big unknown is  what mortgage rates will be like in  three years there's still no shortcut to  qualifying and in this case paying a  premium to punt that into the future  ann gaviola global news toronto  next a mysterious fireball lights up  alberta's night sky  beauty is in the eye of the beholder  even with camels every year saudi arabia  hosts a camel beauty pageant and this  year it has cracked down on enhancements  40 camels were reportedly disqualified  from this year's contest they were  judged to have received botox injections  and facelifts and other artificial  touch-ups jurors are supposed to choose  the winner based on the shape of the  camel's heads necks humps stress and  postures but there is a 66 million  dollar prize at stake so you can see why  some camel owners try to push nature  along  some albertans got an unexpected light  show last night cameras caught something  bright and mysterious streaking across  the sky and there was a loud boom some  are wondering if it was a meteorite as  heather urix west explains it wouldn't  be the first  i was just getting ready for bed and  i heard her rumbling caitlyn kostanuk  isn't sure what her doorbell camera  captured in the skies over central  alberta tuesday night my husband and i  immediately went to meteorite  that's what we thought was right off the  hot the  flash came from behind the neighbor's  house whatever it was mandy mcclanahan  heard it too i was watching tv in my  living room with my cat  we were just chilling it was close to 10  and we heard a huge boom my windows all  shook it almost certainly is a meteor  it's not space debris it was moving  certainly too fast for that fireballs  like this one aren't uncommon in  february a bright light streaked across  the skies above calgary edmonton even  saskatoon  a flash followed by a boom can indicate  parts of the meteor have made it way  down to land these pieces were found by  university of calgary researchers in  2017  but in october one hit way too close to  home for ruth hamilton it sounded like  an explosion happening jumped out of bed  ran over and turned on the light and saw  a hole in my roof the space rock landed  on a pillow just centimeters from her  sleeping head  this time around mandy mcclanahan is  grateful it was just some light and  sound didn't hit the house and didn't  hit the cars so we are good heather urex  west global news calgary  and that is global national for this  wednesday i'm donna friesen tonight's  york canada is the port de grey  peninsula in newfoundland and labrador  all decked out for the holidays we'd  love to see your canada especially  festive and joyful pictures this time of  year please email yours to viewers at  globalnational.com  thanks for watching hope to see you here  again tomorrow take care of yourselves  bye-bye  [Music]  [Applause]  [Music]  you
 

